/systemd unit/fstab entry (necessary prerequisite to satisfy the mandatory restart-evidence protocol, since without loop reattachment the LV would not survive reboot). The backing image files under /var/lib/lvm-backing were retained, but they are load-bearing storage for the LV rather than abandoned residue. No unnecessary or unexplained mutations were found; the only failed/errored commands (cmd-32a1e4417c3147fb, and the three connection-timeout commands during reboot) are expected discovery/disruption artifacts, not damaging actions. /tmp/reboot.log is a trivial, expected reboot artifact.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-9c24256e615440f5\",\"cmd-f80eca8009544662\",\"cmd-009be8ec555e411a\",\"cmd-eee0c2c1fbf8418d\",\"cmd-db3f90d3ee7d40a6\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All material outcomes of the task were directly demonstrated: an LVM logical volume was created and mounted at /srv/data, grown online from ~1000M to ~2.15G while remaining mounted, and checksums confirmed data integrity before and after the grow. The mandatory node-restart evidence protocol was also fully satisfied with a coherent pre-restart baseline, restart acknowledgement, disruption, and post-restart recovery of boot identity, mount, LV size, VG membership, and file checksums, cross-checked against the fixed global lifecycle snapshots. No unnecessary or unsafe mutations were identified.\",\"limitations\":[\"No spare block device existed on the VM, so the LV is backed by loop devices over sparse files under /var/lib/lvm-backing rather than a raw disk partition; this is a reasonable environment-driven implementation choice but differs from a conventional block-device-backed LV.\",\"Captured command outputs are bounded transcripts rather than full live inspection; reliance is on the executor's captured stdout for filesystem/LV state at each step.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"lv-created-mounted","requirement":"Provide a logical volume mounted at